rigin":"inherited","consequence":"frameshift","key":"V6"},{"gene":"CACNA1A","as_reported":"c.5476delC, p.(His1826Thrfs*30)","cdna":"c.5476delC","protein":"p.(His1826Thrfs*30)","genomic":"","zygosity":"compound_heterozygous","origin":"inherited","consequence":"frameshift","key":"V7"}],"variant_roles":["causal","causal"],"segregation":"same compound heterozygous genotype as index sib","inheritance":"AR","previously_reported":"","mixed_genotypes":false,"quotes":["Child II-2 | Died at the age of 14 months, M | Compound heterozygous p.(Ala868Profs*24)/ p.(His1826Thrfs*30) | 5 months | Hypsarrythmia, deep apnoic spells | Severe developmental impairment | Blindness, severe muscular hypotonia, edema, optic atrophy, swallowing disorder (tube feeding) | DEE | PB, LEV, TPM, pyridoxine HCI, CZB, DEX, VGB | Cerebellar hypoplasia","Exome sequencing also confirmed these two variants in material from deceased child II-2.","Based on clinical symptoms, PEHO (progressive encephalopathy, edema, hypsarrhythmia, optic atrophy) syndrome was suspected."],"quote_grounding":[1.0,1.0,1.0],"followup_of":"","genotype_mode":"AR","genotype_reason":"biallelic variants"},{"id":"R:39416668:3","pmid":"39416668","label":"Child II-1","kind":"individual","family":"","n_affected":1,"n_families":1,"sex":"male","ancestry":"","consanguinity":"no","onset":"neonatal","age_last":"died at 5 months","phenotypes":[{"text":"neonatal-onset muscular hypotonia","hpo_label":"Neonatal hypotonia","hpo_id":"HP:0001319","present":true,"n_with":null,"n_assessed":null,"some_only":false,"other_cause":false},{"text":"severe developmental impairment","hpo_label":"Global developmental delay","hpo_id":"HP:0001263","present":true,"n_with":null,"n_assessed":null,"some_only":false,"other_cause":false},{"text":"sudden infant death","hpo_label":"Sudden death","hpo_id":"HP:0001699","present":true,"n_with":null,"n_assessed":null,"some_only":false,"other_cause":false}],"variants":[],"variant_roles":[],"segregation":"not tested; material not available","inheritance":"AR","previously_reported":"","mixed_genotypes":false,"quotes":["The male infant born as the first child of seemingly healthy young, non-consanguineous parents died at the age of 5 months with suspicion of sudden infant death of infancy (SIDS). Neonatal-onset muscular hypotonia was noted","Material of child II-1 was not available for testing."],"quote_grounding":[1.0,1.0],"followup_of":"","genotype_mode":"AR","genotype_reason":"stated for the family"},{"id":"R:39416668:4","pmid":"39416668","label":"Child II-3","kind":"individual","family":"","n_affected":1,"n_families":1,"sex":"male","ancestry":"","consanguinity":"no","onset":"5 years","age_last":"10 years","phenotypes":[{"text":"primary generalized epilepsy","hpo_label":"Generalized-onset seizure","hpo_id":"HP:0002197","present":true,"n_with":null,"n_assessed":null,"some_only":false,"other_cause":false},{"text":"atypical absences","hpo_label":"Atypical absence seizure","hpo_id":"HP:0007270","present":true,"n_with":null,"n_assessed":null,"some_only":false,"other_cause":false},{"text":"mild intellectual impairment","hpo_label":"Mild intellectual disability","hpo_id":"HP:0001256","present":true,"n_with":null,"n_assessed":null,"some_only":false,"other_cause":false},{"text":"episodic unsteady gait","hpo_label":"Episodic ataxia","hpo_id":"HP:0002131","present":true,"n_with":null,"n_assessed":null,"some_only":false,"other_cause":false},{"text":"dizziness","hpo_label":"","hpo_id":"","present":true,"n_with":null,"n_assessed":null,"some_only":false,"other_cause":false},{"text":"ADHD","hpo_label":"Attention deficit hyperactivity disorder","hpo_id":"HP:0007018","present":true,"n_with":null,"n_assessed":null,"some_only":false,"other_cause":false},{"text":"mild muscular hypotonia","hpo_label":"Hypotonia","hpo_id":"HP:0001252","present":true,"n_with":null,"n_assessed":null,"some_only":false,"other_cause":false},{"text":"normal cranial MRI","hpo_label":"Abnormal brain morphology","hpo_id":"HP:0012443","present":false,"n_with":null,"n_assessed":null,"some_only":false,"other_cause":false}],"variants":[{"gene":"CACNA1A","as_reported":"c.2602delG, p.(Ala868Profs*24)","cdna":"c.2602delG","protein":"p.(Ala868Profs*24)","genomic":"","zygosity":"heterozygous","origin":"inherited","consequence":"frameshift","key":"V6"}],"variant_roles":["uncertain"],"segregation":"heterozygous for maternal variant (table lists paternal c.5476delC)","inheritance":"AD","previously_reported":"","mixed_genotypes":false,"quotes":["Child II-3 was found to be heterozygous for the maternal CACNA1A variant c.2602delG, p.(Ala868Profs*24).","Child II-3 | 10 years, M | Heterozygous c.5476delC p.(His1826Thrfs*30) | 5 years (age at last visit 10 years) | Primary generalized epileptic discharges; generalized seizures, atypical absences | Mild intellectual impairment | Ataxia, mild moderate muscular hypotonia, ADHD, dizziness | PGE | LEV | Normal"],"quote_grounding":[1.0,1.0],"followup_of":"","genotype_mode":"AD","genotype_reason":"single heterozygous variant; mode as stated for the family"}],"notes":[{"id":"N:39416668:1","pmid":"39416668","section":"inheritance","statement":"Mammadova et al. (2024) noted that heterozygous parents and a heterozygous sib of patients with biallelic CACNA1A frameshift variants were asymptomatic or had milder neurologic phenotypes; the mother and a maternal uncle carrying c.2602delG had episodic ataxia type 2, whereas the father carrying c.5476delC was asymptomatic. They concluded that CACNA1A belongs to genes with both autosomal dominant and autosomal recessive disease.","inheritance":"AR","quotes":["Similarly to previous reports with early onset epileptic encephalopathy, heterozygous parents and one heterozygous sibling in the herewith reported family were either asymptomatic or presented with milder neurological and/or cognitive symptoms.","CACNA1 therefore belongs to the growing list of genes with co-dominantly or both autosomal dominantly and autosomal recessively inherited diseases.","episodic ataxia type 2 was diagnosed in the mother and one of her brothers due to a heterozygous frameshift pathogenic variant in the CACNA1A gene [c.2602delG, p.(Ala868Profs*24)]."],"quote_grounding":[1.0,1.0,1.0]},{"id":"N:39416668:2","pmid":"39416668","section":"genotype_phenotype_correlations","statement":"Mammadova et al. (2024) suggested that biallelic truncating CACNA1A variants are associated with severe early-onset epileptic encephalopathy with fatal outcome, supporting a correlation between deleteriousness of biallelic loss-of-function variants and severity, compared with longer survival in patients with missense/truncating compound heterozygosity and late-onset myoclonic epilepsy with a homozygous in-frame variant.","inheritance":"AR","quotes":["Our finding of bi-allelic truncating variants being associated with a severe, early onset epileptic encephalopathy with fatal outcome supports a genotype–phenotype correlation regarding deleteriousness of bi-allelic LOF variants and severity of the phenotype."],"quote_grounding":[1.0]},{"id":"N:39416668:3","pmid":"39416668","section":"clinical_features","statement":"Mammadova et al. (2024) proposed that ictal apneic spells were the most prominent seizure type and the probable cause of death in their sibs with biallelic CACNA1A variants, and that the sib diagnosed with SIDS probably died of ictal apnea.","inheritance":"AR","quotes":["ictal apneic spells associated with subtle orofacial (child II-2) or epileptic spasms and generalized clonic seizures (child II-4) were the most prominent seizure type and most probably the cause of death in both brothers with the proven pathogenic variants."],"quote_grounding":[1.0]},{"id":"N:36063114:1","pmid":"36063114","section":"clinical_features","statement":"Wong-Spracklen et al. (2022) reviewed reported biallelic CACNA1A cases and identified consistent features including infantile hypotonia, treatment-refractory seizures with onset in the first 6 months of life, cerebral visual impairment, profound developmental delay with persistent motor impairment, and progressive cerebellar changes on MRI.","inheritance":"AR","quotes":["Consistent features which may aid variant interpretation and clinical management are infantile hypotonia, treatment‐refractory seizures with onset during first 6 months of life, cerebral visual impairment, profound developmental delay with prominent and persistent motor impairments, and progressive cerebellar changes on MRI."],"quote_grounding":[1.0]},{"id":"N:36063114:2","pmid":"36063114","section":"genotype_phenotype_correlations","statement":"Wong-Spracklen et al. (2022) suggested a dose-dependent effect of calcium channel dysfunction: monoallelic CACNA1A variants are associated with a highly variable spectrum from asymptomatic to motor-only syndromes to epilepsy and cognitive impairment, whereas biallelic variants lead to severe, progressive neurologic symptoms and cognitive disability.","inheritance":"UNKNOWN","quotes":["Collectively, these cases suggest a dose‐dependent effect of calcium channel dysfunction: monoallelic variants are associated with a highly variable spectrum ranging from asymptomatic to motor‐only syndromes to epilepsies and cognitive impairment; biallelic variants lead to severe and progressive neurological symptoms and cognitive disabilities."],"quote_grounding":[1.0]},{"id":"N:36063114:3","pmid":"36063114","section":"inheritance","statement":"Wong-Spracklen et al. (2022) noted that, in contrast to previously reported biallelic cases, the heterozygous carrier parents of their patient did not show clear signs of a CACNA1A-associated syndrome, complicating segregation analysis and genetic counseling, with residual uncertainty about recurrence risk (25% or 50%).","inheritance":"AR","quotes":["In contrast to previously reported biallelic cases, parents carrying these monoallelic variants did not display clear signs of a CACNA1A-associated syndrome.","Biallelic variants could be identified by chance, leaving residual uncertainty about recurrence risk (either 25% or 50%, depending on true pathogenicity of both variants)."],"quote_grounding":[1.0,1.0]},{"id":"N:34267336:1","pmid":"34267336","section":"inheritance","statement":"Arteche-López et al. (2021) reported the first patients with a homozygous loss-of-function CACNA1A variant, who had a more severe clinical phenotype than the typical dominant form. The authors suggested that co-dominant or recessive inheritance could explain the phenotypic differences from the dominant CACNA1A phenotypes.","inheritance":"AR","quotes":["All patients presented with a more severe clinical phenotype than the corresponding typical dominant form.","We suggest that co-dominant or recessive inheritance for these three genes could explain the phenotypic differences from those documented in their cognate dominant phenotypes."],"quote_grounding":[1.0,1.0]},{"id":"N:27250579:1","pmid":"27250579","section":"molecular_genetics","statement":"Reinson et al. (2016) found that the parents and two sibs of the compound heterozygous patients were heterozygous for one of the CACNA1A mutations and had a milder phenotype, including intellectual disability; the mother also had adult-onset cerebellar ataxia with slowly progressive cerebellar atrophy.","inheritance":"AD","quotes":["Their parents and two siblings were heterozygotes for one of those pathogenic mutations and expressed a milder phenotype. Both of them have intellectual disability and in addition the mother has adult onset cerebellar ataxia with a slowly progressive cerebellar atrophy."],"quote_grounding":[1.0]},{"id":"N:27250579:2","pmid":"27250579","section":"description","statement":"Reinson et al. (2016) concluded that compound heterozygous CACNA1A mutations presumably cause early-onset epileptic encephalopathy and progressive cerebral, cerebellar, and optic nerve atrophy with reduced lifespan.","inheritance":"AR","quotes":["Compound heterozygous mutations in the CACNA1A gene presumably cause early onset epileptic encephalopathy, and progressive cerebral, cerebellar and optic nerve atrophy with reduced lifespan."],"quote_grounding":[1.0]}],"dropped":{"records":[],"notes":[]},"gene_decisions":{"candidates":["CACNA1A"],"confirmed":["CACNA1A"],"dropped":[],"not_confirmed":[]},"candidate_inheritance":["AR"]}